olumn \\(x=i\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) and choose any subset  \n\n\\[\nS\\subset\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad |S|=k .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\\{L_i\\mid i\\in S\\}\\cup\\{V_i\\mid i\\notin S\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(L_i\\)’s) and \\(n-k\\) vertical lines.\n\n**Coverage.**  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\notin S\\)*, the vertical line \\(V_a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) and directly covers \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\in S\\)*, the vertical line \\(V_a\\) is absent.  \nThe point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\); this line meets the sunny line \\(L_{a-1}\\) (when \\(a>1\\)) at the point \\(P_a=(a,n+1-a)\\).  \nSince \\(L_{a-1}\\) is a straight line passing through the two extreme points \\((a-1,n+2-a)\\) and \\((a,n+1-a)\\), it contains **every** integer point whose first coordinate is \\(a\\) and whose second coordinate lies between \\(1\\) and \\(n+1-a\\).  \nHence \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(L_{a-1}\\) (for \\(a>1\\)).  \nFor the special case \\(a=1\\) we have \\(L_1\\) itself, which contains the points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,n-1)\\); together with the vertical line \\(V_2\\) (present because \\(2\\notin S\\) when \\(1\\in S\\)) all points with first coordinate \\(1\\) are covered.  \n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered by at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\n**Distinctness.**  \nAll vertical lines have slope \\(\\infty\\); the sunny lines have distinct finite slopes \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) \\((i\\in S)\\).  Hence no two lines of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) coincide.\n\nConsequently, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) we have exhibited a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n-----------------------------------------------------------