(V_a\\) is absent, but the two consecutive sunny lines \\(L_{a-1}\\) (when \\(a>1\\)) and \\(L_a\\) together cover the whole column \\(x=a\\):\n     *\\(L_{a-1}\\) passes through the points \\((a-1,n+2-a)\\) and \\((a,n+1-a)\\);  \n     *\\(L_{a}\\) passes through the points \\((a,n+1-a)\\) and \\((a+1,n-a)\\).  \n\n     Because the three points \\((a-1,n+2-a),\\;(a,n+1-a),\\;(a+1,n-a)\\) are collinear with slope \\(\\frac{a-1}{a}\\), the line \\(L_{a-1}\\) contains **every** lattice point whose first coordinate is \\(a\\) and whose second coordinate satisfies \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\).  Hence each point of the column \\(x=a\\) lies on \\(L_{a-1}\\) (for \\(a>1\\)).  \n\n     For the special column \\(a=1\\) we use \\(L_{1}\\) itself; its two defining points are \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,n-1)\\), and the same collinearity argument shows that \\(L_{1}\\) contains all points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).\n\n   Consequently every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\n5. **Distinctness of the lines.**  \n   All vertical lines have slope \\(\\infty\\); the sunny lines have distinct finite slopes \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) \\((i\\in S)\\).  Hence no two lines of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) coincide.\n\nThus for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, and the trivial upper bound shows that larger \\(k\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \