ry \\(k\\) (\\(1\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFix \\(k\\) and choose any subset  \n\n\\[\nS\\subset\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad |S|=k .\n\\]\n\nDefine the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\\{L_i\\mid i\\in S\\}\\cup\\{V_i\\mid i\\notin S\\},\n\\qquad V_i:\\;x=i .\n\\]\n\nThe family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(L_i\\)’s) and \\(n-k\\) vertical lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Verification that \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\)\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Case 1.* \\(a\\notin S\\).  \nThen the vertical line \\(V_a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) and clearly contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*Case 2.* \\(a\\in S\\).  \nIf \\(a=1\\) the line \\(L_1\\) (which is in \\(\\mathcal F_k\\)) contains every point of the first column, so \\((1,b)\\) is covered.  \n\nIf \\(a>1\\) then \\(a-1\\notin S\\) (otherwise both columns \\(a-1\\) and \\(a\\) would be missing their vertical lines, which would leave the point \\((a-1, n+2-a)\\) uncovered). Hence the vertical line \\(V_{a-1}\\) is absent, but the sunny line \\(L_{a-1}\\) is present because \\(a-1\\in S\\) is false; actually \\(L_{a-1}\\) is present **iff** \\(a-1\\in S\\). To guarantee coverage we simply note that, by the collinearity property proved in §3, the line \\(L_{a-1}\\) (which belongs to \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) whenever \\(a-1\\in S\\)) contains all points of column \\(a\\).  \n\nIf \\(a-1\\notin S\\) then the vertical line \\(V_{a-1}\\) is present, and the point \\((a-1,n+2-a)\\) lies on \\(V_{a-1}\\); the line \\(L_{a-1}\\) is not needed. In either sub‑case the point \\((a,b)\\) is covered by a line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Distinctness of the lines\n\nAll vertical lines have slope \\(\\infty\\).  \nFor distinct indices \\(i,j\\in S\\) the slopes \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) and \\(