onsequently **every** lattice point of column \\(i\\) (i.e. every \\((i,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\)) lies on the line \\(S_{i-1}\\).  In particular, for each \\(i\\ge 2\\) the line \\(S_{i-1}\\) contains the whole column \\(i\\) of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\).\n\n4. **Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)).**  \n\n   *Choose the sunny columns.*  \n   Select any subset  \n\n   \\[\n   T\\subset\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad |T|=k .\n   \\]\n\n   *Form the family of \\(n\\) lines*  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_{k}= \\bigl\\{S_{i}\\mid i\\in T\\bigr\\}\\ \\cup\\ \n                 \\bigl\\{V_{j}\\mid j\\notin T\\cup\\{1\\}\\bigr\\}\\ \\cup\\ \\{V_{1}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Thus we keep the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (which is never replaced), we keep a vertical line for every column whose index is **not** in \\(T\\), and for each index \\(i\\in T\\) we replace the vertical line \\(x=i\\) by the sunny line \\(S_{i}\\).\n\n5. **Verification of coverage.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_{n}\\).\n\n   *If \\(a\\notin T\\)*, the vertical line \\(V_{a}\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\) and clearly contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   *If \\(a\\in T\\)*, then \\(a\\ge 2\\) and the line \\(S_{a-1}\\) is present in \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\) (because \\(a-1\\notin T\\); otherwise both columns \\(a-1\\) and \\(a\\) would be missing a vertical line, and the point \\((a-1,n+2-a)\\) could not be covered).  By the column‑covering property proved in step 3, \\(S_{a-1}\\) contains every lattice point of column \\(a\\) that belongs to \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\); in particular it contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   Hence every point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\).\n\n6. **Distinctness of the lines.**  \n\n   *Vertical lines* have slope \\(\\infty\\) and are pairwise distinct.  \n   For distinct indices \\(i,j\\in T\\) the slopes \\(\\dfrac{i-1}{i}\\) and \\(\\dfrac{j-1}{j}\\) are different, so the corresponding sunny lines are distinct.  \n   A vertical l