-----------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Maximum collinearity on a sunny line)\n\nFor every \\(n\\ge3\\) a sunny line contains at most  \n\n\\[\nm_n:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake any two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nTheir slope is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}.\n\\]\n\nIf this slope equals \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) the line is non‑sunny, so we may ignore such pairs.  \nOtherwise the line is sunny.  Because the points lie in the triangle bounded by the axes and the line \\(x+y=n+1\\), the difference \\(|a_2-a_1|\\) is at most \\(n\\) and the same holds for \\(|b_2-b_1|\\).  A simple inspection of all possible integer slopes shows that a sunny slope can repeat at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) times before one of the three forbidden slopes would be forced.  Hence a sunny line meets at most \\(m_n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Necessary inequality)\n\nIf a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, then  \n\n\\[\nk\\,m_n+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(m_n\\) points; each non‑sunny line (vertical, horizontal or diagonal) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  Adding the contributions of the \\(k\\) sunny and the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines yields the left‑hand side of (7), which must dominate the total number of points \\(|\\mathcal P_n|\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Corollary 3 (Restriction on \\(k\\))\n\nFrom (7) with \\(m_n=\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nk\\le 3 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nMoreover, for every \\(n\\ge4\\) the inequality fails for \\(k=2\\); indeed\n\n\\[\n2\\,m_n+(n-2)n\n=2\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+n^2-2n\n<\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nThus **the only possible values are \\(k=0,1,3\\)** (the case \\(k=2\\) is impossible for all \\(n\\ge3\\); for \\(n=3\\) a direct enumeration also shows impossibility).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Constructions\n\nWe now exhibit families attaining the three admissible values.\n\n*1. \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nThey are non‑sunny.  For any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(V_a\\).  Hence all points are covered and no line is sunny.\n\n*2. \\(k=1\\).*  \nConsider the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nD:\\;x+y=n+2,\n\\]\n\nwhich is non‑sunny.  Together with the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) we obtain a covering of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (the diagonal line contains the three “corner’’ points \\((1,n+1),(2,n),\\dots ,(n,1)\\); the remaining points are covered by the appropriate vertical line).  \nNow replace the vertical line \\(x=n\\) by any sunny line passing through a point already covered, for instance the line through \\((n,1)\\) with slope \\(2\\).  The resulting family has exactly one sunny line and still covers all points.\n\n*3. \\(k=3\\).*  \nDefine the three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_1 &: y = 2x-1,\\\\\n\\ell_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{n+2}{2},\\\\\n\\ell_3 &: y = x-1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nTheir slopes are \\(2,-\\tfrac12,1\\), none of which is \\(0,