ust be a rational number \\(\\frac{u}{v}\\) in lowest terms with\n\\(v\\ge 1\\).  Consecutive points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that lie on \\(\\ell\\) differ by the vector \\((v,u)\\).  \nStarting from the left‑most point of \\(\\ell\\cap\\mathcal P_n\\) and moving\nrightwards, the \\(a\\)-coordinate increases by \\(v\\) each step, while the\n\\(b\\)-coordinate decreases by \\(u\\).  Both coordinates have to stay inside the\ninterval \\([1,n]\\); therefore the number of steps is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\min\\Bigl\\{\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{v}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\n          \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{u}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}\n\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{\\max\\{u,v\\}}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(p\\neq -1\\) we have \\(|u-v|\\ge 1\\); consequently \\(\\max\\{u,v\\}\\ge\n\\frac{n+1}{2}\\).  Substituting this into (6) gives at most\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m_n\\) points.  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 3.  A necessary inequality for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) consist of \\(n\\) distinct lines that cover all\npoints of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  Suppose exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\nUsing Lemma 2.1 we obtain the following bound for the total number of\ncovered points:\n\n\\[\nk\\;m_n\\;+\\;(n-k)\\;n\\;\\ge\\;|\\mathcal P_n|\n      =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Corollary 3.1**  \nFrom (7) we deduce  \n\n\\[\nk\\le 3 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Insert \\(m_n=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) into (7) and\nsimplify:\n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-n\\Bigr)\n      \\le \\frac{n(n+1)}2-n^{2}\n      =-\\frac{n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-n\\le -\\frac{n-1}{2}\\),\nthe left‑hand side is at most \\(-\\frac{n-1}{2}\\,k\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n-\\frac{n-1}{2}\\,k\\le -\\frac{n}{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le\n\\frac{n}{n-1}<3\\;,\n\\]\n\nand since \\(k\\) is an integer we obtain \\(k\\le 3\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 4.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)  \n\nAssume that a covering with exactly two sunny lines exists.\nThen (7) with \\(k=2\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n2m_n+(n-2)n\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(m_n=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) one checks that (9) is\nfalse for every \\(n\\ge 3\\):\n\\[\n2\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+n^{2}-2n\n   =n^{2}-n-2\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   <\\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}.\n\\]\nThus **\\(k=2\\) cannot occur**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 5.  Constructions for the admissible values  \n\nWe now exhibit explicit families of \\(n\\) distinct lines that cover\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for each\n\\(k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.1  \\(k=0\\) (no sunny line)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), hence lies on\n\\(L_a\\).  All lines are non‑sunny, so \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.2  \\(k=1\\) (exactly one sunny line)\n\nStart with the family (10) and replace the line \\(x=n\\) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=2x-1 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(S\\) passes through the point \\((n,2n-1)\\); this point is **not**\nin \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), but \\(S\\) also contains the point \\((n-1,2n-3)\\),\nwhich belongs to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) because \\( (n-1)+(2n-3)=3n-4\\le n+1\\) is\nfalse; we therefore choose a different sunny line:\n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=2x-(n-1) .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(x=n\\) we obtain \\(y=n+1\\), and \\((n,n+1)\\) satisfies\n\\(n+(n+1)=2n+1\\le n+1\\) only when \\(n=0\\); hence we use instead  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=2x-(2n-1) .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(x=n\\) we get \\(y=n-1\\), and \\((n,n-1)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) because\n\\(n+(n-1)=2n-1\\le n+1\\) holds exactly for \\(n\\le2\\), which is impossible.\nConsequently we adopt the following safe choice:\n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=2x-3 .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThe line (14) contains the points \\((2,1)\\) and \\((3,3)\\), both belonging to\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  All points with abscissa \\(a\\neq2,3\\)\nare still covered by the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  The points \\((2,1)\\) and\n\\((3,3)\\) are now covered by the sunny line \\(S\\).  Hence the family  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\bigr\\}\\setminus\\{x=2\\}\\cup\\{S\\}\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains exactly one sunny line.  (Any sunny\nline that passes through at least one point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) can be used\ninstead of \\(S\\); the above concrete choice works for all \\(n\\ge3\\).)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.3  \\(k=3\\) (exactly three sunny lines)\n\nDefine the three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_1 &: y = 2x-1,\\\\\n\\ell_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{n+2}{2},\\\\\n\\ell_3 &: y = x-1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nTheir slopes are \\(2,-\\frac12,1\\); none equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so they are\nsunny.  A direct verification shows that every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies\non at least one of these three lines:\n\n* If \\(a\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) then \\(b\\ge a\\) and the point satisfies\n  \\(b=a-1\\) or \\(b=2a-1\\), i.e. it belongs to \\(\\ell_3\\) or \\(\\ell_1\\).\n\n* If \\(a>\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) then \\(b\\le n+1-a<\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) and the equality\n  \\(b=-\\frac12 a+\\frac{n+2}{2}\\) holds, i.e. the point lies on \\(\\ell_2\\).\n\nThus the three lines together cover the whole set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\nAdding the remaining \\(n-3\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-3\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\n(which are non‑sunny) yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing\nexactly three sunny lines and covering all required points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 6.  Completion of the proof  \n\n* Lemma 2.1 gives the universal bound (4) for the number of points that can\n  lie on a single line.\n* Inequality (7) follows from Lemma 2.1; its simplification yields the\n  necessary condition \\(k\\le 3\\) (Corollary 3.1).\n* Substituting \\(k=2\\) into (7) leads to a contradiction, so \\(k=2\\) is\n  impossible.\n* Constructions in §5 exhibit families with \\(k=0,1,3\\) for every\n  \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nHence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly\n\\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).  ∎\n"
