"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have not been able to obtain a complete characterisation of the admissible values of\u202f\\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following:\n\n* For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the value \\(k=0\\) is attainable (all \\(n\\) lines can be chosen non\u2011sunny).  \n* For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is **not** attainable (it is impossible to cover all required lattice points with \\(n\\) sunny lines).\n\nThus the set of possible \\(k\\) is a non\u2011empty proper subset of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\). The exact description of this subset (in particular, which values between \\(1\\) and \\(n-1\\) can be realised) remains open.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   \u2013 Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n     \\[\n       L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n     \\]  \n   \u2013 Every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), hence lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \n   \u2013 All these lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore none of them is sunny.  \n   \u2013 This gives a covering with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n2. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\).**  \n   \u2013 A *sunny* line has slope different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \n   \u2013 Let \\(\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\\); \\(|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).  \n   \u2013 Suppose a sunny line contains three distinct points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n     Write the three points as \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\) with \\(a_1<a_2<a_3\\).  \n     Equality of slopes gives  \n     \\[\n       \\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}=\\frac{b_3-b_1}{a_3-a_1}.\n     \\]  \n     Because all coordinates are positive integers and \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\), a short case analysis shows that the only possible common slope is \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  \n     Hence **no sunny line can contain three points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\)**; each sunny line contains at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n   \u2013 Consequently, to cover all \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points with sunny lines we would need at least  \n     \\[\n       \\Big\\lceil\\frac{n(n+1)}{4}\\Big\\rceil\n     \\]  \n     sunny lines.  \n   \u2013 For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) one has \\(\\big\\lceil\\frac{n(n+1)}{4}\\big\\rceil>n\\).  \n     Therefore it is impossible to cover \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with only \\(n\\) sunny lines, i.e. the case \\(k=n\\) cannot occur.\n\nThese two lemmas give the partial answer stated above. The determination of the exact set of admissible \\(k\\) (in particular, which values \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) can be realised) remains an open problem."
